#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""Read-only control service for the USB 16x16 web UI.

Serves the static UI and a small JSON API describing the running stack. It is
the "read-only backend" step of work/web-ui/README.md: live health, logs and
durable presets, with no endpoint that can change device state itself.

The one exception, added 2026-09-11 as step 5 (staged Apply), keeps that
boundary: POST /api/apply/profile writes a request file into a spool
directory and nothing else. A root systemd path unit runs usb16x16-apply on
it, which validates the request against the live configuration, switches the
AES67 service between ACCEPTED profiles only, verifies the start, rolls back
on failure and leaves a receipt this process reads back. This process still
runs with NoNewPrivileges, still opens no device and still restarts nothing.

Design constraints this file honours:

  * One collector thread owns every Pi read. HTTP handlers copy a cached
    snapshot and never shell out, so a slow or duplicated browser cannot
    multiply work against the audio stack.
  * Three refresh tiers, slowest first, so the daemon API is polled at 1/10 Hz
    and the cheap file reads at 1 Hz.
  * /api/sink/status/N is never read. Its arrival-lead minimum resets on read
    and the latency harness owns that counter.
  * No ALSA device is opened. Meters therefore report unavailable rather than
    inventing a value or competing for a PCM.
  * Runs unprivileged. Nothing here needs root and nothing here is given it;
    the Apply request is a file, and the privilege lives in usb16x16-apply.
  * HTTP Basic auth guards every route. Verifying a password costs a real KDF,
    so a successful credential is cached briefly and repeated failures are rate
    limited per client: the 2 s health poll must not turn into a PBKDF2 load on
    the machine running the audio.
"""

